**Proof.**

We show no such injective map exists.

Recall the definition: for an injective $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$, the pullback metric is
$$f^{*}d^{1}(x, y) := d^{1}(f(x), f(y)) = |f(x) - f(y)|.$$
The requirement $f^{*}d^{1} = d^{2}$ means
$$|f(x) - f(y)| = \|x - y\| \quad \text{for all } x, y \in \mathbb{R}^{2},$$
i.e. $f$ is an isometric embedding of $(\mathbb{R}^{2}, \|\cdot\|)$ into $(\mathbb{R}, |\cdot|)$.

**Key observation.** In $(\mathbb{R}, |\cdot|)$, any three points are collinear (trivially, since $\mathbb{R}$ is a line). Concretely, for any $a, b, c \in \mathbb{R}$, one of the three points lies between the other two, so the three distances $\{|a-b|, |b-c|, |a-c|\}$ always satisfy the *degenerate triangle equality*: one of them equals the sum of the other two.

**Deriving a contradiction.** Consider three specific points in $\mathbb{R}^{2}$:
$$O = (0,0), \quad A = (1,0), \quad B = (0,1).$$
Their pairwise Euclidean distances are
$$d^{2}(O,A) = 1, \qquad d^{2}(O,B) = 1, \qquad d^{2}(A,B) = \sqrt{2}.$$

If $f$ were an isometric embedding, setting $a = f(O)$, $b = f(A)$, $c = f(B)$, we would need
$$|a - b| = 1, \qquad |a - c| = 1, \qquad |b - c| = \sqrt{2}.$$

Since $a, b, c \in \mathbb{R}$, one of them must lie between the other two, so one of the following must hold:
$$|a-b| + |b-c| = |a-c|, \quad \text{or} \quad |a-c| + |c-b| = |a-b|, \quad \text{or} \quad |b-a| + |a-c| = |b-c|.$$

Substituting the required values $1, 1, \sqrt{2}$:
- $1 + \sqrt{2} = 1$? No, since $\sqrt{2} > 0$.
- $1 + \sqrt{2} = 1$? No.
- $1 + 1 = \sqrt{2}$? No, since $2 \neq \sqrt{2}$.

All three possibilities fail. This contradicts the betweenness property that any three real numbers must satisfy.

**Conclusion.** No injective map $f: \mathbb{R}^{2} \to \mathbb{R}$ can satisfy $f^{*}d^{1} = d^{2}$, because the Euclidean plane contains triangles (e.g. the right isosceles triangle with legs $1, 1$ and hypotenuse $\sqrt{2}$) that are non-degenerate, while every triple of points in $\mathbb{R}$ forms a degenerate (collinear) triangle. $\blacksquare$

